1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:03,956
You want to learn Linux, but you do not want to break your own computer.

2
00:00:04,506 --> 00:00:07,493
The answer is a disposable Linux virtual machine.

3
00:00:08,043 --> 00:00:10,539
Break it, delete it, and start fresh in minutes.

4
00:00:11,089 --> 00:00:14,481
Today: why, your options, the install, and how to log in.

5
00:00:16,531 --> 00:00:18,088
First, why disposable?

6
00:00:18,638 --> 00:00:23,332
A virtual machine is separate from your real computer, so a mistake stays inside it.

7
00:00:23,882 --> 00:00:27,082
It is the same for everyone, so your steps match the lesson.

8
00:00:27,632 --> 00:00:29,936
And if it breaks, you delete it, and rebuild.

9
00:00:30,486 --> 00:00:32,491
Treat it like a sandbox, not a home.

10
00:00:34,541 --> 00:00:36,418
Where does the virtual machine come from?

11
00:00:36,968 --> 00:00:41,491
On your own computer, VirtualBox gives you a window, and a normal installer.

12
00:00:42,041 --> 00:00:45,220
Multipass starts an Ubuntu machine with one command.

13
00:00:45,770 --> 00:00:49,866
On Windows, WSL 2 gives you a light Linux environment.

14
00:00:50,416 --> 00:00:55,920
Or rent one in the cloud, from a provider such as AWS, Azure, or Google Cloud.

15
00:00:56,470 --> 00:00:59,883
Cloud machines can cost money, so delete them when you are done.

16
00:01:00,433 --> 00:01:02,886
Any of them works. Pick one, and stay with it.

17
00:01:04,936 --> 00:01:06,557
Next: how big should it be?

18
00:01:07,107 --> 00:01:07,892
For practice,

19
00:01:07,892 --> 00:01:08,797
two processors,

20
00:01:08,797 --> 00:01:10,245
two gigabytes of memory,

21
00:01:10,245 --> 00:01:13,443
and twenty gigabytes of disk are a comfortable start.

22
00:01:13,993 --> 00:01:16,319
Never give it more than your computer can spare.

23
00:01:16,869 --> 00:01:23,290
Choose a long term support release of Ubuntu Server, because it is widely documented.

24
00:01:23,840 --> 00:01:26,044
In my emulator it is one command: qemu-img snapshot -c,

25
00:01:26,044 --> 00:01:26,324
a name,

26
00:01:26,324 --> 00:01:27,046
and the disk file.

27
00:01:27,046 --> 00:01:28,448
The -l option lists your snapshots.

28
00:01:30,498 --> 00:01:34,701
Before the wizard, you need two downloads. First, VirtualBox itself.

29
00:01:35,251 --> 00:01:39,304
Step one: open your web browser and go to virtualbox.org.

30
00:01:39,854 --> 00:01:42,201
Step two: click the big Download button.

31
00:01:42,751 --> 00:01:44,767
Step three: choose your computer.

32
00:01:44,767 --> 00:01:45,440
On Windows,

33
00:01:45,440 --> 00:01:46,723
choose Windows hosts.

34
00:01:46,723 --> 00:01:47,823
On a Mac or Linux,

35
00:01:47,823 --> 00:01:48,617
choose yours.

36
00:01:49,167 --> 00:01:53,391
Run the file you download. You do not need the Extension Pack for this lab.

37
00:01:53,941 --> 00:01:57,077
Your version number will be newer than mine, and that is fine.

38
00:01:59,127 --> 00:02:01,090
Second, the Ubuntu Server image.

39
00:02:01,640 --> 00:02:05,356
Open ubuntu.com slash download slash server,

40
00:02:05,356 --> 00:02:06,961
and press Download.

41
00:02:06,961 --> 00:02:09,917
Choose the Intel or AMD 64 bit one.

42
00:02:10,467 --> 00:02:13,688
It is about three gigabytes, so start it on a good connection.

43
00:02:14,238 --> 00:02:15,573
The file ends in ISO.

44
00:02:15,573 --> 00:02:18,496
That is a disk image the installer boots from.

45
00:02:18,496 --> 00:02:20,276
Remember where you saved it.

46
00:02:20,826 --> 00:02:26,884
At recording time, the latest long term support release was Ubuntu 26.04.1.

47
00:02:27,434 --> 00:02:31,040
Yours may be newer. Choose the current long term support release.

48
00:02:33,090 --> 00:02:35,010
Now the install, in two ways.

49
00:02:35,560 --> 00:02:39,512
With VirtualBox on Windows: run the installer you downloaded,

50
00:02:39,512 --> 00:02:41,068
and accept the defaults.

51
00:02:41,068 --> 00:02:43,530
It may briefly interrupt your network,

52
00:02:43,530 --> 00:02:45,279
and ask to install drivers.

53
00:02:45,279 --> 00:02:45,992
Allow them.

54
00:02:46,542 --> 00:02:50,169
Then open VirtualBox, and create the machine. We do that next.

55
00:02:50,719 --> 00:02:51,144
Later,

56
00:02:51,144 --> 00:02:53,127
inside the Ubuntu installer,

57
00:02:53,127 --> 00:02:55,749
choose a throwaway user and password,

58
00:02:55,749 --> 00:02:58,228
and tick the OpenSSH server option.

59
00:02:58,778 --> 00:03:01,274
With Multipass, one command does all of it.

60
00:03:01,824 --> 00:03:03,381
Launch creates the machine,

61
00:03:03,381 --> 00:03:05,284
shell opens a terminal inside it,

62
00:03:05,284 --> 00:03:07,072
and list shows what is running.

63
00:03:09,122 --> 00:03:11,362
Let us walk through the VirtualBox wizard.

64
00:03:11,912 --> 00:03:16,883
This is a real screen recording of VirtualBox. Follow along on your own computer.

65
00:03:17,433 --> 00:03:19,166
Step one: name the machine,

66
00:03:19,166 --> 00:03:20,001
choose Linux,

67
00:03:20,001 --> 00:03:21,477
and the Ubuntu version.

68
00:03:21,477 --> 00:03:24,174
This is also where you pick your ISO file.

69
00:03:28,724 --> 00:03:32,969
Step two: memory and processors. Two gigabytes, and two processors.

70
00:03:35,519 --> 00:03:37,285
Step three: the virtual disk.

71
00:03:37,285 --> 00:03:38,319
Twenty gigabytes.

72
00:03:38,319 --> 00:03:39,719
It grows as you use it,

73
00:03:39,719 --> 00:03:42,154
so it does not take all of that at once.

74
00:03:45,204 --> 00:03:48,063
Step four: the summary. Check it, then press Finish.

75
00:03:51,113 --> 00:03:53,306
The machine now appears in the list,

76
00:03:53,306 --> 00:03:54,037
powered off.

77
00:03:54,037 --> 00:03:54,768
Press Start,

78
00:03:54,768 --> 00:03:57,022
and the installer boots from the ISO.

79
00:03:59,072 --> 00:04:01,702
On your computer you would now press Start.

80
00:04:01,702 --> 00:04:04,393
My workspace cannot run VirtualBox machines,

81
00:04:04,393 --> 00:04:08,245
so next I will show you the same Ubuntu running in an emulator.

82
00:04:08,795 --> 00:04:09,480
On Windows,

83
00:04:09,480 --> 00:04:11,909
you first run the VirtualBox installer.

84
00:04:11,909 --> 00:04:13,778
I have not captured that part,

85
00:04:13,778 --> 00:04:16,518
but the wizard you just saw is very similar.

86
00:04:18,568 --> 00:04:19,922
Before the first start,

87
00:04:19,922 --> 00:04:21,511
a word about the installer.

88
00:04:21,511 --> 00:04:22,983
I cannot capture it here,

89
00:04:22,983 --> 00:04:24,748
so this is a checklist I drew,

90
00:04:24,748 --> 00:04:26,632
in the order the screens appear.

91
00:04:28,182 --> 00:04:33,515
It is all text menus. Use the arrow keys, Tab, and Enter. The mouse will not work.

92
00:04:34,065 --> 00:04:34,882
Pick English,

93
00:04:34,882 --> 00:04:36,202
your keyboard layout,

94
00:04:36,202 --> 00:04:38,024
and the Ubuntu Server option.

95
00:04:38,024 --> 00:04:39,783
Accept the network defaults,

96
00:04:39,783 --> 00:04:42,108
and leave the proxy and mirror alone.

97
00:04:44,658 --> 00:04:45,393
For storage,

98
00:04:45,393 --> 00:04:46,556
use the whole disk.

99
00:04:46,556 --> 00:04:48,577
That is the virtual disk we made,

100
00:04:48,577 --> 00:04:51,271
so nothing on your real computer is touched.

101
00:04:51,821 --> 00:04:53,124
On the profile screen,

102
00:04:53,124 --> 00:04:54,723
choose the server name lab,

103
00:04:54,723 --> 00:04:56,203
a user name like student,

104
00:04:56,203 --> 00:04:58,157
and a password you will remember.

105
00:05:00,207 --> 00:05:02,325
Tick install OpenSSH server.

106
00:05:02,325 --> 00:05:06,030
You will need it for the SSH part of this lesson.

107
00:05:06,030 --> 00:05:07,845
Skip the extra packages.

108
00:05:08,395 --> 00:05:10,318
Wait for the install to finish,

109
00:05:10,318 --> 00:05:11,745
then choose Reboot now.

110
00:05:11,745 --> 00:05:14,785
If it asks you to remove the installation medium,

111
00:05:14,785 --> 00:05:15,840
just press Enter.

112
00:05:17,390 --> 00:05:19,570
Now let us bring a machine to life.

113
00:05:19,570 --> 00:05:23,619
Everything from here is a real Ubuntu twenty four oh four server.

114
00:05:24,169 --> 00:05:25,284
One honest note.

115
00:05:25,284 --> 00:05:28,349
My workspace cannot run VirtualBox machines,

116
00:05:28,349 --> 00:05:31,204
so I run this same Ubuntu in an emulator.

117
00:05:31,204 --> 00:05:35,732
The console inside your VirtualBox window will look very similar.

118
00:05:36,282 --> 00:05:38,360
Pressing Start is the power button.

119
00:05:38,360 --> 00:05:41,982
Lines of text scroll by while the system starts its services.

120
00:05:41,982 --> 00:05:43,526
This takes about a minute,

121
00:05:43,526 --> 00:05:44,773
so I have sped it up.

122
00:05:45,323 --> 00:05:48,928
When you see the word login, the machine is ready and waiting for you.

123
00:05:56,478 --> 00:06:01,726
Time to log in. Type the user name you created during the install. I will use student.

124
00:06:05,276 --> 00:06:06,384
Now the password.

125
00:06:06,384 --> 00:06:08,991
Notice that nothing appears as you type.

126
00:06:08,991 --> 00:06:09,968
That is normal,

127
00:06:09,968 --> 00:06:11,402
Linux hides passwords.

128
00:06:11,402 --> 00:06:12,575
Type it carefully,

129
00:06:12,575 --> 00:06:13,617
and press Enter.

130
00:06:17,667 --> 00:06:18,333
You are in.

131
00:06:18,333 --> 00:06:19,481
Look at the prompt.

132
00:06:19,481 --> 00:06:21,840
It says student at lab: your user name,

133
00:06:21,840 --> 00:06:23,170
then the machine name.

134
00:06:23,170 --> 00:06:26,073
The dollar sign means: I am ready for a command.

135
00:06:27,623 --> 00:06:32,444
Let us ask the machine who we are. The command is who am I, written as one word.

136
00:06:35,494 --> 00:06:39,355
It answers: student. Now ask for the machine's name: hostname.

137
00:06:43,905 --> 00:06:45,550
Now let us walk around.

138
00:06:45,550 --> 00:06:47,481
First question: where am I?

139
00:06:47,481 --> 00:06:48,840
The command is pwd,

140
00:06:48,840 --> 00:06:51,414
which means print working directory.

141
00:06:55,464 --> 00:06:57,038
That is your home folder.

142
00:06:57,038 --> 00:06:59,052
Now list what is inside with ls.

143
00:06:59,052 --> 00:06:59,807
Nothing yet,

144
00:06:59,807 --> 00:07:01,822
because this is a fresh machine.

145
00:07:05,372 --> 00:07:08,956
Now look at the very top of the file system: ls,

146
00:07:08,956 --> 00:07:09,553
a space,

147
00:07:09,553 --> 00:07:10,449
and a slash.

148
00:07:10,449 --> 00:07:12,241
The slash means the top.

149
00:07:12,241 --> 00:07:13,286
These folders,

150
00:07:13,286 --> 00:07:13,958
like etc,

151
00:07:13,958 --> 00:07:14,929
home and var,

152
00:07:14,929 --> 00:07:17,020
exist on every Linux system.

153
00:07:21,070 --> 00:07:26,595
Which Linux is this? Show the start of the file os-release, in the etc folder.

154
00:07:30,645 --> 00:07:36,298
Ubuntu, twenty four oh four. Does the machine have a network? Type ip -br a.

155
00:07:40,348 --> 00:07:45,630
The address starting with 10.0.2 is the machine's own network address.

156
00:07:45,630 --> 00:07:47,893
One more: how much disk space?

157
00:07:47,893 --> 00:07:49,628
Type df -h and a slash.

158
00:07:53,678 --> 00:07:55,278
About three gigabytes here,

159
00:07:55,278 --> 00:07:57,767
because I made a small disk for this demo.

160
00:07:57,767 --> 00:07:59,012
Yours will be twenty.

161
00:08:01,562 --> 00:08:02,849
Before we power down,

162
00:08:02,849 --> 00:08:05,115
let us create something worth saving.

163
00:08:05,115 --> 00:08:05,728
Type echo,

164
00:08:05,728 --> 00:08:07,321
then a sentence in quotes,

165
00:08:07,321 --> 00:08:08,852
then a greater than sign,

166
00:08:08,852 --> 00:08:09,894
then a file name.

167
00:08:09,894 --> 00:08:13,018
The greater than sign sends the text into the file.

168
00:08:18,568 --> 00:08:21,683
Check it with cat, and the file name. There is our sentence.

169
00:08:25,233 --> 00:08:26,708
Now shut down properly.

170
00:08:26,708 --> 00:08:28,503
Never just close the window,

171
00:08:28,503 --> 00:08:30,427
that is like pulling the plug.

172
00:08:30,427 --> 00:08:31,645
Type sudo poweroff.

173
00:08:31,645 --> 00:08:34,274
Sudo means: do this as the administrator,

174
00:08:34,274 --> 00:08:36,134
so it asks for your password.

175
00:08:41,684 --> 00:08:43,967
Messages scroll as the services stop,

176
00:08:43,967 --> 00:08:46,065
and then the machine switches off.

177
00:08:46,065 --> 00:08:47,977
That is what powered off means.

178
00:08:53,527 --> 00:08:56,770
Now the part many beginners have never seen: a snapshot.

179
00:08:57,320 --> 00:08:59,594
Think of a save point in a video game.

180
00:08:59,594 --> 00:09:01,450
You save before the hard level.

181
00:09:01,450 --> 00:09:02,168
If you fail,

182
00:09:02,168 --> 00:09:03,245
you load the save,

183
00:09:03,245 --> 00:09:05,640
and you are back as if nothing happened.

184
00:09:06,190 --> 00:09:08,556
A snapshot does the same for a machine.

185
00:09:08,556 --> 00:09:11,408
It records the disk exactly as it is right now.

186
00:09:11,408 --> 00:09:14,745
A clean one is easiest when the machine is powered off,

187
00:09:14,745 --> 00:09:15,534
so we waited.

188
00:09:17,084 --> 00:09:19,336
In VirtualBox: select your machine,

189
00:09:19,336 --> 00:09:20,816
open the Snapshots tab,

190
00:09:20,816 --> 00:09:21,781
and press Take.

191
00:09:21,781 --> 00:09:23,968
Give it a name you will recognise,

192
00:09:23,968 --> 00:09:25,191
like clean install.

193
00:09:31,741 --> 00:09:35,047
The snapshot appears in the list, with the current state below it.

194
00:09:39,597 --> 00:09:44,505
In my emulator it is one command: qemu-img snapshot -c,

195
00:09:44,505 --> 00:09:45,129
a name,

196
00:09:45,129 --> 00:09:46,735
and the disk file.

197
00:09:46,735 --> 00:09:49,858
The -l option lists your snapshots.

198
00:09:57,408 --> 00:10:01,483
There it is: clean install, saved. Now we are free to experiment.

199
00:10:05,033 --> 00:10:07,337
Power on again. I have sped up the start.

200
00:10:17,887 --> 00:10:19,551
Log in again, the same way.

201
00:10:25,101 --> 00:10:30,925
Now let us make a mistake a beginner might make by accident: delete an important system folder.

202
00:10:33,475 --> 00:10:37,360
The command removes the folder that holds the SSH settings.

203
00:10:37,360 --> 00:10:39,534
Never run this on a real machine.

204
00:10:46,084 --> 00:10:49,817
Look inside it now. No such file or directory. We broke it.

205
00:10:54,367 --> 00:10:57,588
Power off again. Same command: sudo poweroff.

206
00:11:06,138 --> 00:11:08,473
Here is why we took the snapshot.

207
00:11:08,473 --> 00:11:09,464
We restore it.

208
00:11:09,464 --> 00:11:10,455
In VirtualBox,

209
00:11:10,455 --> 00:11:13,144
select the snapshot and press Restore.

210
00:11:13,144 --> 00:11:14,205
In my emulator,

211
00:11:14,205 --> 00:11:17,036
it is the same snapshot command with -a,

212
00:11:17,036 --> 00:11:17,743
for apply.

213
00:11:28,293 --> 00:11:30,341
Power on once more. Sped up again.

214
00:11:40,891 --> 00:11:43,899
Log in, and check. Is the SSH folder back?

215
00:11:51,449 --> 00:11:54,052
It is back. And is our note still there? Show it.

216
00:11:59,602 --> 00:12:00,821
Everything is back.

217
00:12:00,821 --> 00:12:03,323
Both existed when we took the snapshot,

218
00:12:03,323 --> 00:12:05,698
and anything we did after it is gone.

219
00:12:05,698 --> 00:12:07,751
That is the power of a snapshot.

220
00:12:09,301 --> 00:12:13,056
Break things, learn, restore. That is how you practice without fear.

221
00:12:14,606 --> 00:12:16,035
Now: how do you get in?

222
00:12:16,585 --> 00:12:20,873
The console is the machine's own screen. It always works, but it is awkward.

223
00:12:21,423 --> 00:12:25,071
SSH lets you log in from your own terminal, over the network.

224
00:12:25,621 --> 00:12:30,720
SSH needs three things: an address, a user name, and a way to prove who you are.

225
00:12:31,270 --> 00:12:34,874
The best proof is a key pair: a private key that stays secret,

226
00:12:34,874 --> 00:12:37,606
and a public key that you place on the machine.

227
00:12:38,156 --> 00:12:39,692
Never share the private key.

228
00:12:41,742 --> 00:12:46,478
Now let us do the SSH part for real, in a disposable Linux sandbox.

229
00:12:47,028 --> 00:12:49,297
These outputs are real captures.

230
00:12:49,297 --> 00:12:49,652
Here,

231
00:12:49,652 --> 00:12:52,417
the sandbox plays your virtual machine,

232
00:12:52,417 --> 00:12:54,687
and SSH connects back to itself.

233
00:12:55,237 --> 00:12:58,991
First, we create a key pair. The command stays quiet when it works.

234
00:12:59,541 --> 00:13:04,533
The fingerprint identifies the key, and it is safe to share. The private file is not.

235
00:13:05,083 --> 00:13:06,631
Now we log in with the key,

236
00:13:06,631 --> 00:13:08,808
and run three commands: the host name,

237
00:13:08,808 --> 00:13:09,611
the user name,

238
00:13:09,611 --> 00:13:10,929
and the kernel version.

239
00:13:11,479 --> 00:13:16,599
The answers come from the machine we logged in to: its name, its user, and its kernel.

240
00:13:17,149 --> 00:13:20,818
If we try the wrong port, nothing answers: connection refused.

241
00:13:21,368 --> 00:13:25,507
In your own machine, you use its address, and the port is usually twenty-two.

242
00:13:27,557 --> 00:13:29,114
Three messages you will meet.

243
00:13:29,664 --> 00:13:32,208
Connection refused: the machine answered,

244
00:13:32,208 --> 00:13:34,565
but nothing is listening on that port.

245
00:13:34,565 --> 00:13:36,364
Is SSH installed and running,

246
00:13:36,364 --> 00:13:37,728
and is the port right?

247
00:13:38,278 --> 00:13:40,640
Permission denied: the server is there,

248
00:13:40,640 --> 00:13:43,244
but it rejected your key or your user name.

249
00:13:43,244 --> 00:13:43,910
Check both.

250
00:13:44,460 --> 00:13:46,666
Connection timed out: no answer at all.

251
00:13:46,666 --> 00:13:48,588
Check that the machine is running,

252
00:13:48,588 --> 00:13:50,284
and that the address is right.

253
00:13:50,834 --> 00:13:53,480
Read the message, then check one thing at a time.

254
00:13:55,530 --> 00:13:57,151
Now the disposable part.

255
00:13:57,701 --> 00:13:59,322
Before a risky experiment,

256
00:13:59,322 --> 00:14:00,320
take a snapshot.

257
00:14:00,320 --> 00:14:02,813
VirtualBox can save the machine's state,

258
00:14:02,813 --> 00:14:04,123
and restore it later.

259
00:14:04,673 --> 00:14:08,171
With Multipass, delete the machine, purge it, and launch it again.

260
00:14:08,721 --> 00:14:12,988
Keep anything valuable outside the virtual machine, such as scripts in Git.

261
00:14:13,538 --> 00:14:16,951
If you can rebuild it in minutes, you can experiment without fear.

262
00:14:19,001 --> 00:14:20,857
Watch out for four common myths.

263
00:14:21,407 --> 00:14:24,939
Myth one: a virtual machine puts my computer at risk.

264
00:14:24,939 --> 00:14:25,472
In fact,

265
00:14:25,472 --> 00:14:27,871
it is the safer place to experiment.

266
00:14:28,421 --> 00:14:30,995
Myth two: I need a powerful computer.

267
00:14:30,995 --> 00:14:31,551
In fact,

268
00:14:31,551 --> 00:14:34,821
a small Ubuntu Server runs on modest resources.

269
00:14:35,371 --> 00:14:40,512
Myth three: a password is just as good as a key. In fact, keys are much harder to guess.

270
00:14:41,062 --> 00:14:44,404
Myth four: a disposable machine can hold my real work.

271
00:14:44,404 --> 00:14:44,899
In fact,

272
00:14:44,899 --> 00:14:46,199
if you might need it,

273
00:14:46,199 --> 00:14:47,313
keep it elsewhere.

274
00:14:47,863 --> 00:14:49,698
Disposable means replaceable.

275
00:14:51,748 --> 00:14:53,476
Here is how you check your own work.

276
00:14:54,026 --> 00:14:57,205
Imagine a teammate asks you to set up a practice machine.

277
00:14:57,755 --> 00:14:59,653
Explain why it is disposable.

278
00:15:00,203 --> 00:15:02,059
Choose a platform, and say why.

279
00:15:02,609 --> 00:15:04,444
Create it with sensible sizes.

280
00:15:04,994 --> 00:15:07,063
Log in over SSH, with a key.

281
00:15:07,613 --> 00:15:09,341
Verify it with three commands.

282
00:15:09,891 --> 00:15:11,427
Delete it, and rebuild it.

283
00:15:11,977 --> 00:15:13,599
To pass, you need all six.

284
00:15:15,649 --> 00:15:16,524
Your turn.

285
00:15:17,074 --> 00:15:20,034
This lab is unverified until you run it yourself,

286
00:15:20,034 --> 00:15:23,175
because I cannot install a machine on your computer.

287
00:15:23,725 --> 00:15:26,243
One: create a machine with two processors,

288
00:15:26,243 --> 00:15:27,682
two gigabytes of memory,

289
00:15:27,682 --> 00:15:29,421
and twenty gigabytes of disk.

290
00:15:29,971 --> 00:15:32,360
Two: log in over SSH, with a key.

291
00:15:32,910 --> 00:15:34,463
Three: run the host name,

292
00:15:34,463 --> 00:15:35,084
user name,

293
00:15:35,084 --> 00:15:36,823
and kernel version commands,

294
00:15:36,823 --> 00:15:38,500
and write down the answers.

295
00:15:39,050 --> 00:15:41,005
Four: with the machine powered off,

296
00:15:41,005 --> 00:15:41,898
take a snapshot.

297
00:15:41,898 --> 00:15:43,407
Break something on purpose,

298
00:15:43,407 --> 00:15:44,859
then restore the snapshot,

299
00:15:44,859 --> 00:15:46,367
and check that it is fixed.

300
00:15:46,917 --> 00:15:48,517
Mark yourself on the rubric.

301
00:15:49,067 --> 00:15:50,795
Pause the video, and do it now.

302
00:16:04,345 --> 00:16:05,220
Quick check.

303
00:16:05,770 --> 00:16:07,604
Why use a disposable machine?

304
00:16:08,154 --> 00:16:09,690
Name two ways to get one.

305
00:16:10,240 --> 00:16:11,478
What does a key pair do?

306
00:16:12,028 --> 00:16:13,905
What does connection refused mean?

307
00:16:14,455 --> 00:16:18,956
Objective covered: MC02-O05.

308
00:16:19,506 --> 00:16:21,576
Pause the video, and answer out loud.

309
00:16:32,126 --> 00:16:35,326
Next lesson: files, text, encodings, and paths.

310
00:16:35,876 --> 00:16:37,753
Subscribe, so you do not miss it.
